(Keywords leaves: docs/ledger/keywords.md, kinds 300-306, a NEW reserved
 block (surface brief vf-leaves-keya, round three, 2026-08-30 - see the
 reservation table above 'gen dispatch leaf' in gen_core.vox, which the
 master extends at merge). Ten rows across five of the ledger's own
 categories - articles, statement starters, the `and` keyword, reserved
 aliases, and two classes of special word / contextual keywords:
 KEY-02, KEY-04, KEY-11, KEY-44, KEY-45, KEY-49, KEY-50, KEY-53, KEY-54,
 KEY-73. Every claim hand-verified this session against BOTH pinned
 compilers (installed 0.4.14 and the 0.4.15 stack) before being written
 here - see REPORT-LEAVES-KEYWORDS-A.md for the transcripts.

 KEY-16 through KEY-27 (the process-control rows sharing this ledger's
 Statement Starters table - Create/Remove/Change a directory, symlink,
 Mount/Unmount, Pivot root, Execute, fork/reap, Send signal) are NOT in
 this batch: they are docs/ledger/process-control.md's own PRC-* rows,
 already carrying leaves in src/gen_process.vox (kinds 260-268,
 tonight's "process leaves, batch A"), and this surface brief's own
 category list (articles, starters, connectors, reserved aliases,
 contextual keywords) does not name process control. KEY-30, KEY-61,
 KEY-62, KEY-63, KEY-64, KEY-66, KEY-67, KEY-69, KEY-70, KEY-71, KEY-74,
 KEY-88 remain `todo` too - not attempted this batch; KEY-88 in
 particular is 0.4.15-only (auto/enable/disable's un-reservation) and
 excluded by this round's standing rule for such rows.

 KEY-58 was ALSO selected initially and DROPPED after hand-verification
 found it 0.4.15-only: quoting a single-word RESERVED word as a name
 (`'flag'`, `'empty'`) is refused by the installed 0.4.14
 ("Unknown identifier ''empty''" / "Unknown variable: 'flag'") and
 accepted by the 0.4.15 stack - not documented anywhere in the ledger's
 own KEY-58 row, which cites no compiler version. KEY-73 was drawn in
 as the tenth row instead. See the report's Discrepancies-adjacent
 section for the transcript; not added to keywords.md's own
 Discrepancies section by this worker, per PROCEDURE.md's "record, do
 not adjudicate" - flagged for the master.

 A second, narrower discrepancy surfaced while building these leaves and
 shapes every name choice below: a quoted ONE-WORD name does not resolve
 inside a generated program's own `{...}` format slot on the installed
 0.4.14 (`Print "value: {'pantry'}".` -> "Unknown variable: 'pantry'";
 the 0.4.15 stack prints "value: 42") when the name carries no
 possessive property - WITH a property (`'pantry's size`) it resolves
 correctly on both compilers, bare or in a slot. This matches the
 caution already on file in briefs/brief-leaves-common-2026-08-29.md
 point 4 ("a quoted one-word name does NOT resolve inside a `{...}`
 format slot on 0.4.14"); this session's contribution is confirming it
 is specifically the NO-PROPERTY case, and confirming the general
 assert helpers ('gen assert number'/'gen assert true'/'gen assert
 false'/'gen assert text equals', via 'gen buffer slot of') embed their
 bare SUBJECT inside exactly such a slot for the failure message - so
 any of THOSE helpers, called with a bare quoted-one-word pool name as
 subject, would silently fail to compile on 0.4.14 roughly a sixth of
 the time (one third one-word, just over half of those quoted). Every
 leaf below routes its bare (no-property) assert subjects through
 'gen flow witness name' (gen_flow.vox) or this file's own 'gen
 keywords safe name', both of which return the bare spelling whenever
 the drawn name is one word - always legal, since quoting a one-word
 name is optional either way (LANGUAGE.md:760) - so the slot-unsafe
 branch is never reached. Not filed as a new ledger discrepancy by this
 worker - PROCEDURE.md 5's "record, do not adjudicate" - candidate
 recorded in the report for the master.)

(A declarable, bare-Print-safe, and format-slot-safe rendering of a pool
 stem THIS FILE ALSO NEEDS FOR A POSSESSIVE ACCESS - the same rule 'gen
 flow witness name' applies to a freshly drawn name, reapplied here to a
 stem already held for a second purpose ('gen buffer property', which
 works on the raw stem regardless of quoting - see its own comment).
 Call immediately after 'gen buffer name', before any further draw
 overwrites gen_buffer_last_word.)

To 'gen keywords safe name' with a text called stem and a text called 'the raw word'.
    a boolean called 'is one word' is 'gen name is one word' of 'the raw word'.
    If 'is one word' then,
        Return a text, 'the raw word'.
    a text called 'the closed reference' is 'gen buffer reference' of stem.
    Return a text, 'the closed reference'.

(Rework, owner directive 2026-08-30 (steer-1788090031): every number
 literal below whose MAGNITUDE the row's claim does not pin now draws
 from 'gen extreme number' (gen_core.vox:88) rather than a tame 'rng
 below' - full i64 range, including 0, exactly i64::MAX, 2^31, and
 random lengths up to 18 digits - so a huge, boundary-hugging literal
 reaches the compiler as often as a small one. Left tame on purpose:
 anything sizing an allocation (a buffer's declared capacity) - an
 extreme value there risks a real OOM/hang, which is what "not a value
 the claim pins, like a list length of 3" rules out in the owner's own
 words - and the bare-Create default (0), which the claim itself pins,
 not filler.

 Two independent 'gen extreme number' draws collide often enough to
 matter (~3-5%, dominated by the three fixed-value branches landing on
 the same one twice) for the one row here that needs a GUARANTEED
 difference (KEY-45's mismatched trailing subject). This helper makes
 that guarantee without generator-side arithmetic on a possibly-i64::MAX
 text - a text add is not defined, and casting a fresh draw to a number
 just to add 1 risks silently wrapping in the GENERATOR's own
 computation, which would write a wrong expectation into the emitted
 assertion (the "false-finding factory" CLAUDE.md warns about) rather
 than into anything the compiler is being tested on. Two string
 comparisons are enough: if the draw collides, fall back to "1"; if
 THAT also collides (only possible when the other side already was
 "1"), fall back to "2" - "2" cannot equal "1", so this always
 terminates.)

To 'gen extreme number distinct from' with a text called 'the other'.
    a text called candidate is 'gen extreme number'.
    If candidate is 'the other' then,
        Set candidate to "1".
    If candidate is 'the other' then,
        Set candidate to "2".
    Return a text, candidate.

(A second discovery from the same rework: a BARE number literal alone
 inside a generated program's own `{...}` format slot does not parse -
 `Print "{93918697}".` fails "Unknown variable: 93918697", though the
 identical literal used directly as a comparison operand
 (`If x is not 93918697 then`) or combined with an operator inside the
 slot (`{93918697 add 0}`) both work fine - hand-verified this session.
 'gen assert number matches' wraps its "expected expression" argument in
 exactly such a slot for the failure message ('gen buffer slot of'),
 which is correct when that argument is a RUNTIME property expression
 (`x's size`) but wrong when it is already a compile-time literal, as
 every extreme-number draw below is. 'gen assert number is' does not
 have this problem - it interpolates the NUMBER at the GENERATOR level
 into plain text, with no second-level slot around it in the emitted
 source - so every extreme-literal assertion below casts the drawn text
 to a number (`... as a number`, the same idiom 'gen leaf value retype
 sum' in gen_collections.vox already uses) and calls 'gen assert number
 is', reserving 'gen assert number matches' for the buffer/list `'s
 length` vs `'s size` checks, which really are runtime property
 expressions.)

(KEY-02 - LANGUAGE.md:5039-5040: `a`/`an` declare, `the` references an
 already-declared variable, both as a plain read and as an assignment
 target. Retained probe (KEY-02.vox) hand-verifies one bare name;
 hand-verified further this session, both compilers: `the` also resolves
 a quoted MULTI-WORD name the same way, in a plain statement and inside
 a `{...}` slot. The quoted ONE-WORD shape is deliberately excluded here
 - see this file's header comment - so the name is drawn through 'gen
 flow witness name', which never returns that shape.)

To 'gen leaf article the reference' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called name is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the first value' is 'gen extreme number'.
    a text called 'the second text' is 'gen extreme number distinct from' of 'the first value'.
    a number called 'the second value' is 'the second text' as a number.
    a text called 'the subject' is "the {name}".
    a text called 'the assertion' is 'gen assert number is' of "KEY-02" and 'the subject' and 'the second value'.
    a list called 'the lines' is ["a number called {name} is {'the first value'}", "Set the {name} to {'the second text'}", 'the assertion'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(KEY-04 - LANGUAGE.md:5047: `Set` and `Create` both start a variable
 declaration - `Set a TYPE called NAME to VALUE.`, `Create a TYPE called
 NAME to VALUE.`, and bare `Create a TYPE called NAME.` for the type's
 default. Retained probe (KEY-04.vox) hand-verifies all three shapes for
 `number`/`text`; the bare form's default (0) is claimed here for
 `number` only - other types' defaults are not probed and this leaf does
 not assert them.)

To 'gen leaf set create declaration forms' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the number name' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the text name' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the default name' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the set text' is 'gen extreme number'.
    a number called 'the set value' is 'the set text' as a number.
    a text called 'the text payload' is 'gen text payload'.
    a text called 'the set assertion' is 'gen assert number is' of "KEY-04" and 'the number name' and 'the set value'.
    a text called 'the create assertion' is 'gen assert text equals' of "KEY-04" and 'the text name' and 'the text payload'.
    a text called 'the default assertion' is 'gen assert number is' of "KEY-04" and 'the default name' and 0.
    a list called 'the lines' is ["Set a number called {'the number name'} to {'the set text'}", 'the set assertion', "Create a text called {'the text name'} to \"{'the text payload'}\"", 'the create assertion', "Create a number called {'the default name'}", 'the default assertion'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(KEY-11 - LANGUAGE.md:5054: `Decrement` subtracts 1 from a variable.
 Retained probe (KEY-11.vox) already exercises the bare name and the
 `the`-prefixed form together on one variable; this leaf follows the
 same shape with a random name and starting value.)

To 'gen leaf decrement' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called name is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the start text' is 'gen extreme number'.
    a number called 'the start value' is 'the start text' as a number.
    a number called 'the expected value' is 'the start value' minus 2.
