(KEY-44, KEY-45 - LANGUAGE.md:5121-5123 and 5126: `and` before the final
 subject of a comma-separated subject list terminates the list, and the
 predicate after `are` applies to every subject (KEY-44); disambiguation
 1 says the word after that `and` is read as a SUBJECT, not a condition
 (KEY-45), proven with a bare number as the trailing subject - a
 differing final subject correctly breaks the "are" match, which it
 could not do if it were silently dropped or misread as a boolean
 operand. Retained probes KEY-44.vox and KEY-45.vox hand-verify both
 halves; re-verified this session, both compilers.)

To 'gen leaf and subject list' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the first flag' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the second flag' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the third flag' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the true witness' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the negated witness' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the true check' is "If {'the first flag'}, {'the second flag'}, and {'the third flag'} are true then, Set {'the true witness'} to true".
    a text called 'the true assertion' is 'gen assert true' of "KEY-44" and 'the true witness'.
    a text called 'the negated check' is "If {'the first flag'}, {'the second flag'}, and {'the third flag'} are not false then, Set {'the negated witness'} to true".
    a text called 'the negated assertion' is 'gen assert true' of "KEY-44" and 'the negated witness'.
    a text called 'the matching value' is 'gen extreme number'.
    a text called 'the differing value' is 'gen extreme number distinct from' of 'the matching value'.
    a text called 'the first number' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the second number' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the third number' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the subject witness' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    a text called 'the subject check' is "If {'the first number'}, {'the second number'}, and {'the third number'} are {'the matching value'} then, Set {'the subject witness'} to true".
    a text called 'the subject assertion' is 'gen assert false' of "KEY-45" and 'the subject witness'.
    a list called 'the lines' is ["a boolean called {'the first flag'} is true", "a boolean called {'the second flag'} is true", "a boolean called {'the third flag'} is true", "a boolean called {'the true witness'} is false", 'the true check', 'the true assertion', "a boolean called {'the negated witness'} is false", 'the negated check', 'the negated assertion', "a number called {'the first number'} is {'the matching value'}", "a number called {'the second number'} is {'the matching value'}", "a number called {'the third number'} is {'the differing value'}", "a boolean called {'the subject witness'} is false", 'the subject check', 'the subject assertion'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(KEY-49, KEY-50 - LANGUAGE.md:5239-5240: `message` and `string` are
 reserved aliases of the type name `text`. A variable declared with
 either spelling is a text: same reported type, same content. Retained
 probes KEY-49.vox and KEY-50.vox hand-verify both against a bare
 one-word name; re-verified this session, both compilers, including the
 `'s type` possessive on a quoted stem.)

To 'gen leaf reserved alias text types' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called 'the message stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the message raw' is gen_buffer_last_word.
    a text called 'the message name' is 'gen keywords safe name' of 'the message stem' and 'the message raw'.
    a text called 'the string stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the string raw' is gen_buffer_last_word.
    a text called 'the string name' is 'gen keywords safe name' of 'the string stem' and 'the string raw'.
    a text called 'the message payload' is 'gen text payload'.
    a text called 'the string payload' is 'gen text payload'.
    a text called 'the message type subject' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the message stem' and "type".
    a text called 'the message type assertion' is 'gen assert text equals' of "KEY-49" and 'the message type subject' and "Text (static)".
    a text called 'the message content assertion' is 'gen assert text equals' of "KEY-49" and 'the message name' and 'the message payload'.
    a text called 'the string type subject' is 'gen buffer property' of 'the string stem' and "type".
    a text called 'the string type assertion' is 'gen assert text equals' of "KEY-50" and 'the string type subject' and "Text (static)".
    a text called 'the string content assertion' is 'gen assert text equals' of "KEY-50" and 'the string name' and 'the string payload'.
    a list called 'the lines' is ["a message called {'the message name'} is \"{'the message payload'}\"", "a string called {'the string name'} is \"{'the string payload'}\"", 'the message type assertion', 'the message content assertion', 'the string type assertion', 'the string content assertion'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(KEY-53, KEY-54 - LANGUAGE.md:5310-5314: `size` and its synonym `length`
 are contextual keywords - the property after a possessive, but also a
 legal bare variable name, both in the same program; `x's length` still
 means the same as `x's size`. Retained probes KEY-53.vox and KEY-54.vox
 hand-verify a variable literally named `length` alongside a buffer's
 and a list's `'s length`/`'s size` agreement; re-verified this session,
 both compilers. `length` is the contextual keyword itself, not a pool
 draw - the row's claim is specifically about that spelling.)

